---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import DownloadScripts from '../components/DownloadScripts.astro';
import { base } from '../lib/base.js';
import { products } from '../lib/marks.js';
import { download, downloads as loadDownloads, github, pages, setupYaml, workbenchNav } from '../lib/workbench.js';
// Written by screenshots/capture.cjs: the overview's numbered regions and
// the toolbar strip's crops and hotspots, as percentages of each image.
import screenshots from '../data/screenshots.json';

const downloads = await loadDownloads();
const version = downloads && downloads.version;
const mark = products.find(p => p.name === 'Workbench');

const statesYaml = `- label: Sign in
  src: pages/sign-in.html
  states:
    - id: default
      label: Default
    - id: error
      label: Wrong password`;
---
<Base
  title="Workbench by Canonic"
  description="A design workbench for the screens in your repo: every page and preview on one canvas in VS Code, marked up and handed to your agent."
  product="Workbench"
  version={version}
  nav={workbenchNav('Overview')}
  github={github}
  download={download}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <a class="chip" href="#tour">
          <svg viewBox={mark.viewBox} aria-hidden="true"><path d={mark.base} fill="currentColor" /><path d={mark.accent} class="accent" /></svg>
          Canonic Workbench{version && ` v${version}`}
        </a>
        <h1>Every screen in your repo, on one canvas.</h1>
        <p>A VS Code extension for reviewing screens and components. Define TypeScript previews or connect your existing pages and tools, see each screen at a real device width, draw on it, and hand the picture to your agent.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href="#install" id="heroDownload">Download for VS Code</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#tour">Take the tour</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={pages.docs}>Read the docs</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="window">
        <div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span>
          <span class="addr">#pages/sign-in.html</span>
        </div>
        <div class="shot pinned">
          <img src={`${base}images/overview.jpg`} width="1440" height="860" alt="Workbench: a screen list on the left with an Auth folder and a Sign in page, and the Acme sign-in page on the canvas at full width.">
          {screenshots.regions.map(region => (
            <>
              <span class="region" style={`left: ${region.box.left}%; top: ${region.box.top}%; width: ${region.box.width}%; height: ${region.box.height}%`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="pin" style={`left: ${region.pin.left}%; top: ${region.pin.top}%`} title={region.name} aria-hidden="true">{region.n}</span>
            </>
          ))}
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tour" class="tour band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="toolbar-block">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="kicker">Tour</span>
          <h2>Four areas, one window.</h2>
          <p class="lede">Workbench reads <code>workbench.yaml</code> at the root of your project, shows discovered TypeScript previews alongside manual pages in the sidebar, and imports the implementation catalogs you enable.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="areas">
          <li><span class="n">01</span><h3>Screen list</h3><p>Laid out like Sketch’s sidebar: the sections in <code>workbench.yaml</code>, such as Pages and Components, listed on top, and the chosen one’s screens and folders below, with search at the bottom. A screen with states lists them underneath it. In VS Code this list sits in the sidebar, where the file tree usually goes, under a switcher for the projects you’ve added, such as a product and its design system.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">02</span><h3>Top bar</h3><p>The screen and its state on the left, with the other states a click away, then the Actions toggle. Lenses sit in the middle; frame sizes and screen actions on the right. When space runs short, secondary actions move into the More menu.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">03</span><h3>Canvas</h3><p>The screen itself, live, in a frame of the size you picked. It’s your real page, not a picture of it. Zoom and pan it like a design canvas; the page keeps its real width. Managed previews reuse their renderer when you switch screens. The current screen stays visible while its replacement loads, and offscreen lazy images don’t hold it up.</p></li>
          <li><span class="n">04</span><h3>Markup bar</h3><p>Drawing tools and the handoff, floating under the frame. Select is the one way back to using the page.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="toolbar-block" id="toolbar">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="kicker">Toolbar</span>
          <h2>Every control, in place.</h2>
          <p class="lede">The Actions toggle and the frame and screen controls run across the top of the workbench; the markup tools and the zoom control float over the bottom of the canvas. Point at or tap a control to see what it does.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="strip">
          {screenshots.toolbar.map(group => (
            <div class="strip-group" data-group={group.name}>
              <img src={base + group.src} width={group.width} height={group.height} alt={group.alt}>
              {group.controls.map(control => (
                <button class="hot" type="button" style={`left: ${control.left}%; width: ${control.width}%`} data-label={control.label}></button>
              ))}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="caption" id="toolbarCaption" aria-live="polite">
          <p class="caption-name">Actions</p>
          <p class="caption-text">Off by default, so clicking around a screen you’re reviewing doesn’t navigate you out of it. Turn it on to let links navigate and forms submit, and walk the real flow.</p>
        </div>

        <details class="all-controls">
          <summary>All controls</summary>
          <div class="controls-groups">
            <div class="controls-col">
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Interaction</h3>
                <dl class="controls" data-group="actions">
                  <div data-label="Actions — let links navigate and forms submit"><dt>Actions</dt><dd>Off by default, so clicking around a screen you’re reviewing doesn’t navigate you out of it. Turn it on to let links navigate and forms submit, and walk the real flow.</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Only in some setups</h3>
                <dl class="controls">
                  <div><dt>State picker</dt><dd>Beside the screen’s name in the top bar when it has states, or under a Storybook lens, listing the title’s stories. Story picks reuse the loaded preview.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Lens switcher</dt><dd>Workbench for TypeScript previews, or Design for HTML pages, followed by additional implementations declared in <code>workbench.yaml</code>. See <a href="#lenses">Lenses</a>.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Preview controls</dt><dd>For Workbench previews: edit inputs, reset the selected state, inspect actions, and read documentation. Changes stay temporary.</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>Copy handoff</dt><dd>In VS Code only, at the end of the markup bar, beside a count of your marks. See <a href="#markup">Markup and handoff</a>.</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <div class="controls-group">
                <h3>Zoom</h3>
                <dl class="controls" data-group="zoom">
                  <div data-label="Zoom out"><dt>Zoom out</dt><dd>Halves the zoom, to the next power of two. <kbd>⌘−</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl −</kbd>.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom options"><dt>Zoom level</dt><dd>Opens zoom to fit, zoom to 100%, 50%, and 200%.</dd></div>
                  <div data-label="Zoom in"><dt>Zoom in</dt><dd>Doubles the zoom, to the next power of two. <kbd>⌘+</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl +</kbd>.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="controls-note">As in Figma: <kbd>⌘</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with the scroll wheel, or a pinch, zooms at the pointer. Scroll to pan, or hold <kbd>Space</kbd> and drag. <kbd>⌘0</kbd> or <kbd>⇧0</kbd> zooms to 100%; <kbd>⇧1</kbd> zooms to fit. A new frame size fits itself to the window until you zoom or pan.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="controls-group">
              <h3>Markup</h3>
              <dl class="controls" data-group="markup">
                <div data-label="Select"><dt>Select</dt><dd>Use the page, and pick or move marks. <kbd>Esc</kbd> returns here from any drawing tool.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Scribble"><dt>Scribble</dt><dd>Freehand drawing.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Arrow"><dt>Arrow</dt><dd>Point at something.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Rectangle"><dt>Shapes</dt><dd>Rectangle by default. The small arrow beside it switches to a line or a circle.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Text"><dt>Text</dt><dd>A label placed on the screen.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Comment"><dt>Comment</dt><dd>A pinned note, for feedback that needs more than a word.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Undo last mark"><dt>Undo</dt><dd>Removes the last mark. <kbd>⌘Z</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl Z</kbd>.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Clear markup"><dt>Clear markup</dt><dd>Removes every mark on the screen.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Save screenshot"><dt>Save screenshot</dt><dd>Downloads a JPEG of the screen with your marks on it.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
            <div class="controls-group">
              <h3>Frame and screen</h3>
              <dl class="controls" data-group="frame">
                <div data-label="Fit"><dt>Fit</dt><dd>The frame fills whatever space the workbench has.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Laptop, MacBook Pro 14, 1512 by 982 points"><dt>Laptop</dt><dd>1512 × 982, a 14-inch MacBook Pro. The canvas zooms out to show all of it; the page still lays out at its full width.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Mobile, iPhone 15 Pro, 393 by 852 points"><dt>Mobile</dt><dd>393 × 852, an iPhone 15 Pro.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Resizable viewport"><dt>Resizable</dt><dd>Drag an outside edge or corner to any size. The frame keeps that size until you change it.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Reload"><dt>Reload</dt><dd>Reloads the screen.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Open the source"><dt>Open the source</dt><dd>Lists the screen’s design file and any implementation code, and opens one in the editor.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Copy reference"><dt>Copy reference</dt><dd>Copies a short text reference to the screen, state, and lens, for pasting into a conversation. It doesn’t include a screenshot or your markup.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="Open on its own"><dt>Open on its own</dt><dd>Opens the screen directly in a browser tab.</dd></div>
                <div data-label="More"><dt>More (⋯)</dt><dd><b>Configure pages</b>: a form for adding and removing sections and pages, editing labels and paths, and choosing each page’s viewports; it rewrites only the <code>sections</code> part of <code>workbench.yaml</code>. <b>Download design-system ZIP</b>: every screen’s files, the sources they depend on, and a reference screenshot at each viewport, in parts of 10 MB or less. When the window is narrow, the controls that don’t fit move here too.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="features">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="feature" id="states">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <span class="kicker">States</span>
          <h2>A screen is more than one picture.</h2>
          <p>Sign in has the empty form you land on and the one that comes back with a wrong password. Each state is listed under its screen, the way Storybook lists stories under a component.</p>
          <p>Have the page respond to the state. Match CSS on <code>html[data-wb-state="error"]</code>, keep an element only in some states with <code>data-wb-state-only="error"</code>, or fill in a value with <code>data-wb-set-error="value=…"</code>. Your pages need no scripts added.</p>
          <div class="code">
            <div class="code-head"><span class="code-file">workbench.yaml</span><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy the states example">Copy</button></div>
            <pre><code>{statesYaml}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="feature-media shot">
          <img src={`${base}images/states.jpg`} width="1120" height="860" loading="lazy" alt="The Wrong password state of the sign-in page at mobile width: an error message above filled-in fields, with the password field outlined in red.">
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="feature" id="markup">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <span class="kicker">Markup and handoff</span>
          <h2>Draw on the live page. Hand it to your agent.</h2>
          <p>Boxes, arrows, scribbles, and pinned comments sit above the running screen, so you can keep using it with Select. Select a mark to move it, nudge it with the arrow keys, or delete it with <kbd>Delete</kbd>.</p>
          <p>In VS Code, <b>Copy handoff</b> saves the screenshot to the project’s ignored <code>.canonic/.handoffs/</code> folder, clears the markup, and copies a written account of every mark to the clipboard. The account says what’s under each mark and which file the screen comes from. Paste it into any agent conversation.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="feature-media shot">
          <img src={`${base}images/markup.jpg`} width="1120" height="860" loading="lazy" alt="The error state with markup: a red box around the error message, an arrow pointing to it, and a wavy line under the password field.">
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="feature" id="lenses">
        <div class="feature-text">
          <span class="kicker">Lenses</span>
          <h2>Your previews, in their own language.</h2>
          <p>Define components and screens in TypeScript <code>.workbench.ts</code> files. The default Workbench lens supports HTML, React, Vue, Astro, and React Native Web, with adapters for other technologies, named states, editable inputs, and an action log. Exports include an independent browser viewer with states, viewports, controls, actions, and docs. Astro exports include the authored states; editing Astro props requires the live Workbench server. Storybook, dev servers, staging, the iOS Simulator, and macOS app windows remain available as additional lenses.</p>
          <p>Like a Storybook story, a preview renders your real page and gives it its data per state: props, providers from a project-wide environment, or mocked responses to the page’s own requests, so a screen that loads data shows its empty, loading, and error states unchanged. Its links open other previews, and anything else it would do on a live site, such as a download or another route, is logged as an action instead. See <a href={`${pages.docs}preview-data/#choose-how-to-give-a-screen-its-data`}>Give a screen its data</a>.</p>
          <p>The choice stays as you move between screens, and the address carries it, so a copied link opens the same view. A project can also configure a start command and a readiness check for a Storybook or local server; in a trusted VS Code workspace, Workbench starts it in a terminal only when the check fails. The canvas opens immediately with a loading indicator while services start, and the sidebar names the catalogs it is waiting for.</p>
          <img src={`${base}images/workbench-previews.jpg`} alt="Acme TypeScript preview with its named states and editable input controls" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="feature-media">
          <div class="code">
            <div class="code-head"><span class="code-file">address</span><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy the address example">Copy</button></div>
            <pre><code>#pages/sign-in.html:error@393~staging</code></pre>
          </div>
          <table class="address">
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>pages/sign-in.html</code></td><td>the screen’s design file</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>:error</code></td><td>the state, left out for the default</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>@393</code></td><td>the frame: a device width, <code>fit</code>, or a resizable size</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>~staging</code></td><td>the lens, left out for the design</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="setup" class="setup ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="setup-text">
        <span class="kicker">Setup</span>
        <h2>Start with one configuration file.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The tool ships inside the extension. Workbench turns on in a folder with a <code>workbench.yaml</code>; your pages and optional TypeScript preview definitions stay in your project.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="code-file">workbench.yaml</span><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy the workbench.yaml example">Copy</button></div>
        <pre><code>{setupYaml}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="install-head">
        <div class="section-head">
          <span class="kicker">Install{version && ` · v${version}`}</span>
          <h2>Get Canonic Workbench.</h2>
          {downloads && <p class="lede">One <code>.vsix</code> file per platform. Choose the one that matches your computer.</p>}
        </div>
        <a class="text-link" href={pages.install}>Install guide →</a>
      </div>

      <Install downloads={downloads} />

      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <span class="n">01</span>
          <span class="title">Install the .vsix</span>
          <p>In VS Code, open the Extensions view, click <b>⋯</b>, and choose <b>Install from VSIX…</b>. Or run:</p>
          <span class="cmd"><code data-install-cmd>code --install-extension &lt;file&gt;.vsix</code><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy the install command">Copy</button></span>
        </li>
        <li><span class="n">02</span><span class="title">Reload VS Code</span><p>Run <b>Developer: Reload Window</b> from the Command Palette.</p></li>
        <li><span class="n">03</span><span class="title">Add workbench.yaml</span><p>Create it at the root of your project, like the <a href="#setup">example above</a>.</p></li>
        <li><span class="n">04</span><span class="title">Open the canvas</span><p>Workbench appears in the activity bar, and opens the canvas.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

<script is:inline>
/* The toolbar strip: each hotspot captions itself from the control list, so
   the descriptions live in one place. The last control stays captioned. */
(function () {
  var strip = document.querySelector('.strip');
  var name = document.querySelector('.caption-name');
  var text = document.querySelector('.caption-text');
  if (!strip || !name || !text) return;
  var entries = {};
  document.querySelectorAll('.controls [data-label]').forEach(function (row) {
    entries[row.getAttribute('data-label')] = row;
  });
  var hots = strip.querySelectorAll('.hot');
  hots.forEach(function (hot) {
    var row = entries[hot.getAttribute('data-label')];
    if (!row) { hot.hidden = true; return; }
    hot.setAttribute('aria-label', row.querySelector('dt').textContent);
    hot.setAttribute('aria-describedby', 'toolbarCaption');
  });
  function show(hot) {
    var row = entries[hot.getAttribute('data-label')];
    if (!row || hot.classList.contains('is-active')) return;
    hots.forEach(function (h) { h.classList.toggle('is-active', h === hot); });
    name.textContent = row.querySelector('dt').textContent;
    text.innerHTML = row.querySelector('dd').innerHTML;
  }
  ['pointerover', 'focusin', 'click'].forEach(function (type) {
    strip.addEventListener(type, function (e) {
      var hot = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('.hot');
      if (hot) show(hot);
    });
  });
  if (hots[0]) show(hots[0]);
})();
</script>
<DownloadScripts />

  <Fragment slot="footer"><span>Screens shown are an example project.</span></Fragment>
</Base>
